1
00:00:01,760 --> 00:00:05,119
Chapter 7.

2
00:00:05,119 --> 00:00:09,920
At the time when I stood in the churchyard reading the family tombstones, I had just enough learning

3
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:16,960
to be able to spell them out. My construction even of their simple meaning was not very correct,

4
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:22,800
for I read “wife of the Above” as a complimentary reference to my father’s exaltation to a better

5
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:29,079
world; and if any one of my deceased relations had been referred to as “Below,” I have no doubt

6
00:00:29,079 --> 00:00:33,800
I should have formed the worst opinions of that member of the family. Neither were my notions of

7
00:00:33,800 --> 00:00:39,920
the theological positions to which my Catechism bound me, at all accurate; for, I have a lively

8
00:00:39,920 --> 00:00:46,119
remembrance that I supposed my declaration that I was to “walk in the same all the days of my life,”

9
00:00:46,119 --> 00:00:51,000
laid me under an obligation always to go through the village from our house in one particular

10
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:57,720
direction, and never to vary it by turning down by the wheelwright’s or up by the mill.

11
00:00:57,720 --> 00:01:04,319
When I was old enough, I was to be apprenticed to Joe, and until I could assume that dignity I was

12
00:01:04,319 --> 00:01:11,880
not to be what Mrs. Joe called “Pompeyed,” or (as I render it) pampered. Therefore,

13
00:01:11,880 --> 00:01:17,119
I was not only odd-boy about the forge, but if any neighbour happened to want an extra boy to

14
00:01:17,119 --> 00:01:25,119
frighten birds, or pick up stones, or do any such job, I was favoured with the employment. In order,

15
00:01:25,119 --> 00:01:30,239
however, that our superior position might not be compromised thereby, a money-box was kept

16
00:01:30,239 --> 00:01:34,960
on the kitchen mantel-shelf, into which it was publicly made known that all my earnings were

17
00:01:34,960 --> 00:01:39,920
dropped. I have an impression that they were to be contributed eventually towards the liquidation

18
00:01:39,920 --> 00:01:46,119
of the National Debt, but I know I had no hope of any personal participation in the treasure.

19
00:01:46,119 --> 00:01:50,800
Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt kept an evening school in the village; that is to say,

20
00:01:50,800 --> 00:01:56,159
she was a ridiculous old woman of limited means and unlimited infirmity, who used to go to sleep

21
00:01:56,159 --> 00:02:02,079
from six to seven every evening, in the society of youth who paid two pence per week each,

22
00:02:02,079 --> 00:02:08,520
for the improving opportunity of seeing her do it. She rented a small cottage,

23
00:02:08,520 --> 00:02:13,560
and Mr. Wopsle had the room upstairs, where we students used to overhear him reading aloud in

24
00:02:13,560 --> 00:02:19,039
a most dignified and terrific manner, and occasionally bumping on the ceiling. There

25
00:02:19,039 --> 00:02:24,800
was a fiction that Mr. Wopsle “examined” the scholars once a quarter. What he did on those

26
00:02:24,800 --> 00:02:30,560
occasions was to turn up his cuffs, stick up his hair, and give us Mark Antony’s oration over the

27
00:02:30,560 --> 00:02:35,640
body of Caesar. This was always followed by Collins’s Ode on the Passions, wherein

28
00:02:35,640 --> 00:02:41,960
I particularly venerated Mr. Wopsle as Revenge throwing his blood-stained sword in thunder down,

29
00:02:41,960 --> 00:02:46,920
and taking the War-denouncing trumpet with a withering look. It was not with me then,

30
00:02:46,920 --> 00:02:51,319
as it was in later life, when I fell into the society of the Passions,

31
00:02:51,319 --> 00:02:57,359
and compared them with Collins and Wopsle, rather to the disadvantage of both gentlemen.

32
00:02:57,359 --> 00:03:03,119
Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt, besides keeping this Educational Institution, kept in the same room—a

33
00:03:03,119 --> 00:03:09,039
little general shop. She had no idea what stock she had, or what the price of anything in it was;

34
00:03:09,039 --> 00:03:13,319
but there was a little greasy memorandum-book kept in a drawer, which served as a Catalogue

35
00:03:13,319 --> 00:03:20,600
of Prices, and by this oracle Biddy arranged all the shop transactions. Biddy was Mr. Wopsle’s

36
00:03:20,600 --> 00:03:25,439
great-aunt’s granddaughter; I confess myself quite unequal to the working out of the problem,

37
00:03:25,439 --> 00:03:30,720
what relation she was to Mr. Wopsle. She was an orphan like myself; like me, too,

38
00:03:30,720 --> 00:03:35,560
had been brought up by hand. She was most noticeable, I thought, in respect of her

39
00:03:35,560 --> 00:03:40,920
extremities; for, her hair always wanted brushing, her hands always wanted washing,

40
00:03:40,920 --> 00:03:45,680
and her shoes always wanted mending and pulling up at heel. This description must

41
00:03:45,680 --> 00:03:51,640
be received with a week-day limitation. On Sundays, she went to church elaborated.

42
00:03:52,159 --> 00:03:57,920
Much of my unassisted self, and more by the help of Biddy than of Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt,

43
00:03:57,920 --> 00:04:03,000
I struggled through the alphabet as if it had been a bramble-bush; getting considerably worried and

44
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:09,840
scratched by every letter. After that I fell among those thieves, the nine figures, who seemed every

45
00:04:09,840 --> 00:04:16,840
evening to do something new to disguise themselves and baffle recognition. But, at last I began,

46
00:04:16,840 --> 00:04:24,399
in a purblind groping way, to read, write, and cipher, on the very smallest scale.

47
00:04:24,399 --> 00:04:28,800
One night I was sitting in the chimney corner with my slate, expending great efforts on the

48
00:04:28,800 --> 00:04:34,079
production of a letter to Joe. I think it must have been a full year after our hunt upon the

49
00:04:34,079 --> 00:04:41,279
marshes, for it was a long time after, and it was winter and a hard frost. With an alphabet on the

50
00:04:41,279 --> 00:04:47,920
hearth at my feet for reference, I contrived in an hour or two to print and smear this epistle:—

51
00:04:47,920 --> 00:04:54,840
“Mi deer Jo ,I ope U R krwite well. I ope, I shal son B habell 4 2 teedge U, Jo,

52
00:04:54,840 --> 00:05:03,479
an then we shorl B so glodd. An wen I M prengtd 2 U, Jo, wot larx! An bleve me, inf xn, Pip.”

53
00:05:03,479 --> 00:05:08,199
There was no indispensable necessity for my communicating with Joe by letter,

54
00:05:08,199 --> 00:05:14,079
inasmuch as he sat beside me and we were alone. But I delivered this written communication

55
00:05:14,079 --> 00:05:21,840
(slate and all) with my own hand, and Joe received it as a miracle of erudition.

56
00:05:21,840 --> 00:05:25,600
“I say, Pip, old chap!” cried Joe, opening his blue eyes wide,

57
00:05:25,600 --> 00:05:30,479
“what a scholar you are! An’t you?” “I should like to be,” said I,

58
00:05:30,479 --> 00:05:35,920
glancing at the slate as he held it; with a misgiving that the writing was rather hilly.

59
00:05:35,920 --> 00:05:37,800
“Why, here’s a J,” said Joe,

60
00:05:37,800 --> 00:05:44,359
“and a O equal to anythink! Here’s a J and a O, Pip, and a J-O, Joe.”

61
00:05:44,359 --> 00:05:48,680
I had never heard Joe read aloud to any greater extent than this monosyllable,

62
00:05:48,680 --> 00:05:53,920
and I had observed at church last Sunday, when I accidentally held our Prayer-Book upside down,

63
00:05:53,920 --> 00:05:58,840
that it seemed to suit his convenience quite as well as if it had been all right. Wishing

64
00:05:58,840 --> 00:06:02,800
to embrace the present occasion of finding out whether in teaching Joe,

65
00:06:02,800 --> 00:06:08,760
I should have to begin quite at the beginning, I said, “Ah! But read the rest, Jo.”

66
00:06:08,760 --> 00:06:14,920
“The rest, eh, Pip?” said Joe, looking at it with a slow, searching eye, “One, two,

67
00:06:14,920 --> 00:06:21,439
three. Why, here’s three Js, and three Os, and three J-O, Joes in it, Pip!”

68
00:06:22,479 --> 00:06:27,640
I leaned over Joe, and, with the aid of my forefinger read him the whole letter.

69
00:06:27,640 --> 00:06:33,199
“Astonishing!” said Joe, when I had finished. “You ARE a scholar.”

70
00:06:33,199 --> 00:06:38,279
“How do you spell Gargery, Joe?” I asked him, with a modest patronage.

71
00:06:38,279 --> 00:06:40,680
“I don’t spell it at all,” said Joe.

72
00:06:40,680 --> 00:06:42,560
“But supposing you did?”

73
00:06:42,560 --> 00:06:48,000
“It can’t be supposed,” said Joe. “Tho’ I’m uncommon fond of reading, too.”

74
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:49,800
“Are you, Joe?”

75
00:06:49,800 --> 00:06:56,840
“On-common. Give me,” said Joe, “a good book, or a good newspaper, and sit me down afore a good fire,

76
00:06:56,840 --> 00:07:02,600
and I ask no better. Lord!” he continued, after rubbing his knees a little, “when you do come

77
00:07:02,600 --> 00:07:09,760
to a J and a O, and says you, ‘Here, at last, is a J-O, Joe,’ how interesting reading is!”

78
00:07:09,760 --> 00:07:12,159
I derived from this, that Joe’s education,

79
00:07:12,159 --> 00:07:17,720
like Steam, was yet in its infancy. Pursuing the subject, I inquired,—

80
00:07:17,720 --> 00:07:21,199
“Didn’t you ever go to school, Joe, when you were as little as me?”

81
00:07:21,199 --> 00:07:22,880
“No, Pip.”

82
00:07:22,880 --> 00:07:27,000
“Why didn’t you ever go to school, Joe, when you were as little as me?”

83
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:30,800
“Well, Pip,” said Joe, taking up the poker, and settling himself to his

84
00:07:30,800 --> 00:07:36,600
usual occupation when he was thoughtful, of slowly raking the fire between the lower bars;

85
00:07:36,600 --> 00:07:41,840
“I’ll tell you. My father, Pip, he were given to drink, and when he were overtook with drink,

86
00:07:41,840 --> 00:07:47,920
he hammered away at my mother, most onmerciful. It were a’most the only hammering he did, indeed,

87
00:07:47,920 --> 00:07:53,479
’xcepting at myself. And he hammered at me with a wigor only to be equalled by the wigor with which

88
00:07:53,479 --> 00:07:58,520
he didn’t hammer at his anwil.—You’re a listening and understanding, Pip?”

89
00:07:58,520 --> 00:07:59,960
“Yes, Joe.”

90
00:07:59,960 --> 00:08:05,680
“Consequence, my mother and me we ran away from my father several times; and then my mother she’d

91
00:08:05,680 --> 00:08:11,600
go out to work, and she’d say, “Joe,” she’d say, “now, please God, you shall have some schooling,

92
00:08:11,600 --> 00:08:17,359
child,” and she’d put me to school. But my father were that good in his hart that he couldn’t abear

93
00:08:17,359 --> 00:08:23,000
to be without us. So, he’d come with a most tremenjous crowd and make such a row at the

94
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:28,239
doors of the houses where we was, that they used to be obligated to have no more to do with us and

95
00:08:28,239 --> 00:08:35,680
to give us up to him. And then he took us home and hammered us. Which, you see, Pip,” said Joe,

96
00:08:35,680 --> 00:08:42,439
pausing in his meditative raking of the fire, and looking at me, “were a drawback on my learning.”

97
00:08:42,439 --> 00:08:44,800
“Certainly, poor Joe!”

98
00:08:44,800 --> 00:08:48,680
“Though mind you, Pip,” said Joe, with a judicial touch or two of the poker on

99
00:08:48,680 --> 00:08:55,319
the top bar, “rendering unto all their doo, and maintaining equal justice betwixt man and man,

100
00:08:55,319 --> 00:08:58,760
my father were that good in his hart, don’t you see?”

101
00:08:58,760 --> 00:09:02,399
I didn’t see; but I didn’t say so.

102
00:09:02,399 --> 00:09:06,159
“Well!” Joe pursued, “somebody must keep the pot a-biling,

103
00:09:06,159 --> 00:09:09,279
Pip, or the pot won’t bile, don’t you know?”

104
00:09:09,279 --> 00:09:11,600
I saw that, and said so.

105
00:09:11,600 --> 00:09:16,199
“Consequence, my father didn’t make objections to my going to work; so I went to work at my

106
00:09:16,199 --> 00:09:21,359
present calling, which were his too, if he would have followed it, and I worked tolerable hard,

107
00:09:21,359 --> 00:09:26,720
I assure you, Pip. In time I were able to keep him, and I kep him till he went off in a purple

108
00:09:26,720 --> 00:09:31,760
leptic fit. And it were my intentions to have had put upon his tombstone that,

109
00:09:31,760 --> 00:09:38,520
Whatsume’er the failings on his part, Remember reader he were that good in his heart.”

110
00:09:38,520 --> 00:09:43,600
Joe recited this couplet with such manifest pride and careful perspicuity,

111
00:09:43,600 --> 00:09:47,119
that I asked him if he had made it himself.

112
00:09:47,119 --> 00:09:53,520
“I made it,” said Joe, “my own self. I made it in a moment. It was like striking out a

113
00:09:53,520 --> 00:09:59,159
horseshoe complete, in a single blow. I never was so much surprised in all my life,—couldn’t

114
00:09:59,159 --> 00:10:05,880
credit my own ed,—to tell you the truth, hardly believed it were my own ed. As I was saying,

115
00:10:05,880 --> 00:10:11,840
Pip, it were my intentions to have had it cut over him; but poetry costs money,

116
00:10:11,840 --> 00:10:18,479
cut it how you will, small or large, and it were not done. Not to mention bearers,

117
00:10:18,479 --> 00:10:23,319
all the money that could be spared were wanted for my mother. She were in poor elth, and quite

118
00:10:23,319 --> 00:10:30,359
broke. She weren’t long of following, poor soul, and her share of peace come round at last.”

119
00:10:30,359 --> 00:10:36,439
Joe’s blue eyes turned a little watery; he rubbed first one of them, and then the other,

120
00:10:36,439 --> 00:10:43,000
in a most uncongenial and uncomfortable manner, with the round knob on the top of the poker.

121
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:46,720
“It were but lonesome then,” said Joe, “living here alone,

122
00:10:46,720 --> 00:10:53,640
and I got acquainted with your sister. Now, Pip,”—Joe looked firmly at me as if he knew I

123
00:10:53,640 --> 00:10:59,039
was not going to agree with him;—“your sister is a fine figure of a woman.”

124
00:10:59,039 --> 00:11:03,680
I could not help looking at the fire, in an obvious state of doubt.

125
00:11:03,680 --> 00:11:09,119
“Whatever family opinions, or whatever the world’s opinions, on that subject may be, Pip, your sister

126
00:11:09,119 --> 00:11:17,640
is,” Joe tapped the top bar with the poker after every word following, “a-fine-figure—of—a—woman!”

127
00:11:17,640 --> 00:11:22,640
I could think of nothing better to say than “I am glad you think so, Joe.”

128
00:11:22,640 --> 00:11:29,800
“So am I,” returned Joe, catching me up. “I am glad I think so, Pip. A little redness

129
00:11:29,800 --> 00:11:34,600
or a little matter of Bone, here or there, what does it signify to Me?”

130
00:11:34,600 --> 00:11:40,720
I sagaciously observed, if it didn’t signify to him, to whom did it signify?

131
00:11:40,720 --> 00:11:45,960
“Certainly!” assented Joe. “That’s it. You’re right, old chap! When I got acquainted with

132
00:11:45,960 --> 00:11:50,800
your sister, it were the talk how she was bringing you up by hand. Very kind of her too,

133
00:11:50,800 --> 00:11:58,199
all the folks said, and I said, along with all the folks. As to you,” Joe pursued with a countenance

134
00:11:58,199 --> 00:12:04,359
expressive of seeing something very nasty indeed, “if you could have been aware how small and flabby

135
00:12:04,359 --> 00:12:09,399
and mean you was, dear me, you’d have formed the most contemptible opinion of yourself!”

136
00:12:09,399 --> 00:12:13,399
Not exactly relishing this, I said, “Never mind me, Joe.”

137
00:12:13,399 --> 00:12:18,560
“But I did mind you, Pip,” he returned with tender simplicity. “When I offered

138
00:12:18,560 --> 00:12:23,119
to your sister to keep company, and to be asked in church at such times as she was

139
00:12:23,119 --> 00:12:30,439
willing and ready to come to the forge, I said to her, ‘And bring the poor little child. God

140
00:12:30,439 --> 00:12:36,800
bless the poor little child,’ I said to your sister, ‘there’s room for him at the forge!’”

141
00:12:36,800 --> 00:12:41,159
I broke out crying and begging pardon, and hugged Joe round the neck: who dropped the

142
00:12:41,159 --> 00:12:50,000
poker to hug me, and to say, “Ever the best of friends; an’t us, Pip? Don’t cry, old chap!”

143
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:53,359
When this little interruption was over, Joe resumed:—

144
00:12:53,359 --> 00:12:59,399
“Well, you see, Pip, and here we are! That’s about where it lights; here we are! Now,

145
00:12:59,399 --> 00:13:04,119
when you take me in hand in my learning, Pip (and I tell you beforehand I am awful dull,

146
00:13:04,119 --> 00:13:09,359
most awful dull), Mrs. Joe mustn’t see too much of what we’re up to. It must be done,

147
00:13:09,359 --> 00:13:14,840
as I may say, on the sly. And why on the sly? I’ll tell you why, Pip.”

148
00:13:14,840 --> 00:13:17,039
He had taken up the poker again;

149
00:13:17,039 --> 00:13:21,199
without which, I doubt if he could have proceeded in his demonstration.

150
00:13:21,199 --> 00:13:23,880
“Your sister is given to government.”

151
00:13:23,880 --> 00:13:30,119
“Given to government, Joe?” I was startled, for I had some shadowy idea (and I am afraid I must add,

152
00:13:30,119 --> 00:13:36,960
hope) that Joe had divorced her in a favour of the Lords of the Admiralty, or Treasury.

153
00:13:36,960 --> 00:13:42,840
“Given to government,” said Joe. “Which I meantersay the government of you and myself.”

154
00:13:42,840 --> 00:13:44,319
“Oh!”

155
00:13:44,319 --> 00:13:48,720
“And she an’t over partial to having scholars on the premises,” Joe continued,

156
00:13:48,720 --> 00:13:52,119
“and in partickler would not be over partial to my being a scholar,

157
00:13:52,119 --> 00:13:57,000
for fear as I might rise. Like a sort of rebel, don’t you see?”

158
00:13:57,000 --> 00:14:02,560
I was going to retort with an inquiry, and had got as far as “Why—” when Joe stopped me.

159
00:14:02,560 --> 00:14:05,199
“Stay a bit. I know what you’re a-going to say,

160
00:14:05,199 --> 00:14:09,319
Pip; stay a bit! I don’t deny that your sister comes the Mo-gul over us,

161
00:14:09,319 --> 00:14:14,079
now and again. I don’t deny that she do throw us back-falls, and that she do drop down upon

162
00:14:14,079 --> 00:14:21,399
us heavy. At such times as when your sister is on the Ram-page, Pip,” Joe sank his voice to

163
00:14:21,399 --> 00:14:28,880
a whisper and glanced at the door, “candour compels fur to admit that she is a Buster.”

164
00:14:28,880 --> 00:14:34,680
Joe pronounced this word, as if it began with at least twelve capital Bs.

165
00:14:34,680 --> 00:14:39,640
“Why don’t I rise? That were your observation when I broke it off, Pip?”

166
00:14:39,640 --> 00:14:41,600
“Yes, Joe.”

167
00:14:41,600 --> 00:14:47,239
“Well,” said Joe, passing the poker into his left hand, that he might feel his whisker;

168
00:14:47,239 --> 00:14:51,159
and I had no hope of him whenever he took to that placid occupation;

169
00:14:51,159 --> 00:14:55,880
“your sister’s a master-mind. A master-mind.”

170
00:14:55,880 --> 00:15:01,560
“What’s that?” I asked, in some hope of bringing him to a stand. But Joe was readier

171
00:15:01,560 --> 00:15:07,079
with his definition than I had expected, and completely stopped me by arguing circularly,

172
00:15:07,079 --> 00:15:11,399
and answering with a fixed look, “Her.”

173
00:15:11,399 --> 00:15:16,000
“And I ain’t a master-mind,” Joe resumed, when he had unfixed his look,

174
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:22,199
and got back to his whisker. “And last of all, Pip,—and this I want to say very serious to you,

175
00:15:22,199 --> 00:15:28,600
old chap,—I see so much in my poor mother, of a woman drudging and slaving and breaking her honest

176
00:15:28,600 --> 00:15:33,560
hart and never getting no peace in her mortal days, that I’m dead afeerd of going wrong in the

177
00:15:33,560 --> 00:15:38,760
way of not doing what’s right by a woman, and I’d fur rather of the two go wrong the t’other way,

178
00:15:38,760 --> 00:15:44,079
and be a little ill-conwenienced myself. I wish it was only me that got put out, Pip;

179
00:15:44,079 --> 00:15:49,000
I wish there warn’t no Tickler for you, old chap; I wish I could take it all on myself;

180
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:54,600
but this is the up-and-down-and-straight on it, Pip, and I hope you’ll overlook shortcomings.”

181
00:15:54,600 --> 00:16:00,319
Young as I was, I believe that I dated a new admiration of Joe from that night. We were

182
00:16:00,319 --> 00:16:05,640
equals afterwards, as we had been before; but, afterwards at quiet times when I sat

183
00:16:05,640 --> 00:16:09,840
looking at Joe and thinking about him, I had a new sensation of feeling conscious

184
00:16:09,840 --> 00:16:17,199
that I was looking up to Joe in my heart. “However,” said Joe, rising to replenish the fire;

185
00:16:17,199 --> 00:16:21,600
“here’s the Dutch-clock a-working himself up to being equal to strike Eight of ’em,

186
00:16:21,600 --> 00:16:26,079
and she’s not come home yet! I hope Uncle Pumblechook’s mare mayn’t have set a

187
00:16:26,079 --> 00:16:32,199
forefoot on a piece o’ ice, and gone down.” Mrs. Joe made occasional trips with Uncle

188
00:16:32,199 --> 00:16:36,960
Pumblechook on market-days, to assist him in buying such household stuffs and goods

189
00:16:36,960 --> 00:16:43,840
as required a woman’s judgment; Uncle Pumblechook being a bachelor and reposing no confidences in

190
00:16:43,840 --> 00:16:50,720
his domestic servant. This was market-day, and Mrs. Joe was out on one of these expeditions.

191
00:16:51,880 --> 00:16:55,279
Joe made the fire and swept the hearth, and then we went to the

192
00:16:55,279 --> 00:17:00,680
door to listen for the chaise-cart. It was a dry cold night, and the wind blew keenly,

193
00:17:00,680 --> 00:17:06,119
and the frost was white and hard. A man would die to-night of lying out on the marshes,

194
00:17:06,119 --> 00:17:12,000
I thought. And then I looked at the stars, and considered how awful it would be for a man to turn

195
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:19,119
his face up to them as he froze to death, and see no help or pity in all the glittering multitude.

196
00:17:19,119 --> 00:17:24,319
“Here comes the mare,” said Joe, “ringing like a peal of bells!”

197
00:17:24,319 --> 00:17:28,920
The sound of her iron shoes upon the hard road was quite musical, as she came along

198
00:17:28,920 --> 00:17:35,279
at a much brisker trot than usual. We got a chair out, ready for Mrs. Joe’s alighting,

199
00:17:35,279 --> 00:17:39,920
and stirred up the fire that they might see a bright window, and took a final survey of

200
00:17:39,920 --> 00:17:45,680
the kitchen that nothing might be out of its place. When we had completed these preparations,

201
00:17:45,680 --> 00:17:51,800
they drove up, wrapped to the eyes. Mrs. Joe was soon landed, and Uncle Pumblechook

202
00:17:51,800 --> 00:17:57,760
was soon down too, covering the mare with a cloth, and we were soon all in the kitchen,

203
00:17:57,760 --> 00:18:03,600
carrying so much cold air in with us that it seemed to drive all the heat out of the fire.

204
00:18:03,600 --> 00:18:08,520
“Now,” said Mrs. Joe, unwrapping herself with haste and excitement,

205
00:18:08,520 --> 00:18:13,439
and throwing her bonnet back on her shoulders where it hung by the strings,

206
00:18:13,439 --> 00:18:17,760
“if this boy ain’t grateful this night, he never will be!”

207
00:18:17,760 --> 00:18:20,920
I looked as grateful as any boy possibly could,

208
00:18:20,920 --> 00:18:25,720
who was wholly uninformed why he ought to assume that expression.

209
00:18:25,720 --> 00:18:32,159
“It’s only to be hoped,” said my sister, “that he won’t be Pompeyed. But I have my fears.”

210
00:18:32,159 --> 00:18:38,319
“She ain’t in that line, Mum,” said Mr. Pumblechook. “She knows better.”

211
00:18:38,319 --> 00:18:43,920
She? I looked at Joe, making the motion with my lips and eyebrows,

212
00:18:43,920 --> 00:18:50,640
“She?” Joe looked at me, making the motion with his lips and eyebrows, “She?”

213
00:18:51,159 --> 00:18:55,159
My sister catching him in the act, he drew the back of his hand across his

214
00:18:55,159 --> 00:19:00,560
nose with his usual conciliatory air on such occasions, and looked at her.

215
00:19:00,560 --> 00:19:08,439
“Well?” said my sister, in her snappish way. “What are you staring at? Is the house afire?”

216
00:19:08,439 --> 00:19:12,880
“—Which some individual,” Joe politely hinted, “mentioned—she.”

217
00:19:12,880 --> 00:19:17,520
“And she is a she, I suppose?” said my sister. “Unless you call Miss Havisham

218
00:19:17,520 --> 00:19:22,279
a he. And I doubt if even you’ll go so far as that.”

219
00:19:22,279 --> 00:19:25,720
“Miss Havisham, up town?” said Joe.

220
00:19:25,720 --> 00:19:29,359
“Is there any Miss Havisham down town?” returned my sister.

221
00:19:29,359 --> 00:19:34,279
“She wants this boy to go and play there. And of course he’s going. And

222
00:19:34,279 --> 00:19:37,600
he had better play there,” said my sister, shaking her head at

223
00:19:37,600 --> 00:19:43,840
me as an encouragement to be extremely light and sportive, “or I’ll work him.”

224
00:19:43,840 --> 00:19:48,000
I had heard of Miss Havisham up town,—everybody for miles round

225
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:53,319
had heard of Miss Havisham up town,—as an immensely rich and grim lady who lived in

226
00:19:53,319 --> 00:19:59,960
a large and dismal house barricaded against robbers, and who led a life of seclusion.

227
00:19:59,960 --> 00:20:05,560
“Well to be sure!” said Joe, astounded. “I wonder how she come to know Pip!”

228
00:20:05,560 --> 00:20:09,800
“Noodle!” cried my sister. “Who said she knew him?”

229
00:20:09,800 --> 00:20:13,199
“—Which some individual,” Joe again politely hinted,

230
00:20:13,199 --> 00:20:16,319
“mentioned that she wanted him to go and play there.”

231
00:20:16,319 --> 00:20:20,520
“And couldn’t she ask Uncle Pumblechook if he knew of a boy to go and play there?

232
00:20:20,520 --> 00:20:24,960
Isn’t it just barely possible that Uncle Pumblechook may be a tenant of hers, and

233
00:20:24,960 --> 00:20:30,039
that he may sometimes—we won’t say quarterly or half-yearly, for that would be requiring too much

234
00:20:30,039 --> 00:20:36,560
of you—but sometimes—go there to pay his rent? And couldn’t she then ask Uncle Pumblechook if he knew

235
00:20:36,560 --> 00:20:41,479
of a boy to go and play there? And couldn’t Uncle Pumblechook, being always considerate

236
00:20:41,479 --> 00:20:47,479
and thoughtful for us—though you may not think it, Joseph,” in a tone of the deepest reproach,

237
00:20:47,479 --> 00:20:54,159
as if he were the most callous of nephews, “then mention this boy, standing Prancing here”—which

238
00:20:54,159 --> 00:21:00,079
I solemnly declare I was not doing—“that I have for ever been a willing slave to?”

239
00:21:00,079 --> 00:21:04,479
“Good again!” cried Uncle Pumblechook. “Well put! Prettily pointed! Good

240
00:21:04,479 --> 00:21:11,479
indeed! Now Joseph, you know the case.” “No, Joseph,” said my sister, still in a

241
00:21:11,479 --> 00:21:17,960
reproachful manner, while Joe apologetically drew the back of his hand across and across his nose,

242
00:21:17,960 --> 00:21:24,920
“you do not yet—though you may not think it—know the case. You may consider that you do,

243
00:21:24,920 --> 00:21:30,319
but you do not, Joseph. For you do not know that Uncle Pumblechook, being sensible that

244
00:21:30,319 --> 00:21:35,720
for anything we can tell, this boy’s fortune may be made by his going to Miss Havisham’s,

245
00:21:35,720 --> 00:21:40,800
has offered to take him into town to-night in his own chaise-cart, and to keep him to-night,

246
00:21:40,800 --> 00:21:45,159
and to take him with his own hands to Miss Havisham’s to-morrow morning. And

247
00:21:45,159 --> 00:21:51,560
Lor-a-mussy me!” cried my sister, casting off her bonnet in sudden desperation, “here I stand

248
00:21:51,560 --> 00:21:57,640
talking to mere Mooncalfs, with Uncle Pumblechook waiting, and the mare catching cold at the door,

249
00:21:57,640 --> 00:22:02,800
and the boy grimed with crock and dirt from the hair of his head to the sole of his foot!”

250
00:22:02,800 --> 00:22:08,800
With that, she pounced upon me, like an eagle on a lamb, and my face was squeezed into wooden

251
00:22:08,800 --> 00:22:14,920
bowls in sinks, and my head was put under taps of water-butts, and I was soaped, and kneaded,

252
00:22:14,920 --> 00:22:20,560
and towelled, and thumped, and harrowed, and rasped, until I really was quite beside

253
00:22:20,560 --> 00:22:26,800
myself. (I may here remark that I suppose myself to be better acquainted than any living authority,

254
00:22:26,800 --> 00:22:33,920
with the ridgy effect of a wedding-ring, passing unsympathetically over the human countenance.)

255
00:22:33,920 --> 00:22:38,840
When my ablutions were completed, I was put into clean linen of the stiffest character,

256
00:22:38,840 --> 00:22:45,159
like a young penitent into sackcloth, and was trussed up in my tightest and fearfullest suit.

257
00:22:45,159 --> 00:22:50,159
I was then delivered over to Mr. Pumblechook, who formally received me as if he were the Sheriff,

258
00:22:50,159 --> 00:22:55,760
and who let off upon me the speech that I knew he had been dying to make all along: “Boy,

259
00:22:55,760 --> 00:23:02,119
be forever grateful to all friends, but especially unto them which brought you up by hand!”

260
00:23:02,119 --> 00:23:03,640
“Good-bye, Joe!”

261
00:23:03,640 --> 00:23:06,359
“God bless you, Pip, old chap!”

262
00:23:06,359 --> 00:23:11,520
I had never parted from him before, and what with my feelings and what with soapsuds,

263
00:23:11,520 --> 00:23:17,560
I could at first see no stars from the chaise-cart. But they twinkled out one by one,

264
00:23:17,560 --> 00:23:22,439
without throwing any light on the questions why on earth I was going to play at Miss Havisham’s,

265
00:23:22,439 --> 00:23:24,983
and what on earth I was expected to play at. Chapter 8.
